---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import tracking from '../data/tracking.json';
import retentionData from '../data/retention.json';

const { parties, notPresent, verified, stance } = tracking;

// The comparison this page exists to make. Pulled from the same retention
// data /rules uses, so if those figures are ever corrected this moves with
// them rather than drifting into a stale claim.
const peel = retentionData.entries.find((e: any) => e.operator === 'Peel Regional Police');
const toronto = retentionData.entries.find((e: any) => e.operator === 'Toronto Police Service');
---
<Base
  title="What we track"
  titleFull="What Readback Tracks About You — In Full, With Sources"
  description="Every party that sees you read this site, what each collects, how long they keep it, and the source for each figure. Including where documentation runs out.">
  <h1>What we know about you</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site asks police services what they collect and how long they keep it. It would be
    incoherent not to answer the same question about ourselves — to the same standard, with a
    citation on every line.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>"No analytics" would not have been the same as "no collection."</strong> A site can
    run no analytics at all and still hand a reader's address to its host, its font provider and
    its map provider before the page finishes loading. What follows is everyone who sees
    something when you read this, not just the tool we chose to install.
  </div>

  <h2>{stance.heading}</h2>
  <p class="lede">{stance.lede}</p>
  <div class="tests">
    {stance.tests.map((t: any) => (
      <div class="test">
        <h3>{t.q}</h3>
        <p>{t.a}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="close">{stance.close}</p>

  <h2>The comparison that matters</h2>
  <p>
    We hold this site to the standard we apply to police services, so here is the honest version
    of that comparison. It is not flattering in every direction, which is the point.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Keeps data on</th><th>For how long</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {peel && <tr><td>{peel.j}</td><td>Drivers who matched nothing</td><td><strong>{peel.r}</strong></td></tr>}
        {toronto && <tr><td>{toronto.j}</td><td>Drivers who matched nothing</td><td><strong>{toronto.r}</strong></td></tr>}
        <tr class="us">
          <td>Readback (via Google Analytics)</td>
          <td>Readers of this site</td>
          <td><strong>2 years</strong>, as a cookie on your device — which you can delete</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="us">
          <td>Readback (via Google Analytics)</td>
          <td>Readers of this site</td>
          <td><strong>2 months</strong>, as event and user data on Google's servers</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="meta">
    A cookie lifetime is not the same thing as a plate database, and we are not going to pretend
    it is. But the number is longer than either force keeps a licence plate, and a page that
    hid that while running this site's argument would deserve the challenge it got.
  </p>
  <div class="note control-note">
    <strong>The difference is who holds it.</strong> Our two-year figure is a cookie sitting on
    <em>your</em> device, and you can delete it right now — no request, no form, no waiting.
    Do that and we are back to knowing nothing about you. You cannot delete a plate read out of
    a police database; there is no button, and the only route in is a freedom-of-information
    request that takes months and may come back redacted. That asymmetry is the entire reason
    this site exists, and it is why we would rather publish this page than be caught omitting it.
    <span class="control-links">
      <a href="https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/95647" rel="noopener">Delete, allow and manage cookies in Chrome →</a>
      · <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="noopener">Opt out of Google Analytics entirely →</a>
    </span>
  </div>

  <h2>Everyone who sees something</h2>
  {parties.map((p: any) => (
    <section class="party">
      <div class="party-head">
        <h3>{p.name}</h3>
        <span class={`need ${p.necessity === 'Chosen' ? 'chosen' : p.necessity === 'On by default' ? 'default' : 'required'}`}>{p.necessity}</span>
        <span class="scope">{p.scope}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="role">{p.role}</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Why it is here</dt>
        <dd>{p.necessityWhy}</dd>
        <dt>What it collects</dt>
        <dd>
          {p.collects}
          {p.collectsQuote && (
            <blockquote>
              “{p.collectsQuote}”
              <cite>— {p.collectsQuoteSource}</cite>
            </blockquote>
          )}
        </dd>
        <dt>How long it is kept</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class={`retention ${p.retentionFlag}`}>{p.retention}</span>
          <span class="detail">{p.retentionDetail}</span>
          {p.ourClaim && (
            <p class="our-claim"><strong>Take our word for it, and note that you have to.</strong> {p.ourClaim}</p>
          )}
        </dd>
        <dt>What we can see</dt>
        <dd>{p.weSee}</dd>
        {p.control && (
          <>
            <dt>What you can do about it</dt>
            <dd>
              {p.control.text}
              <span class="control-links">
                {p.control.links.map((l: any, i: number) => (
                  <>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={l.url} rel="noopener">{l.label} →</a></>
                ))}
              </span>
            </dd>
          </>
        )}
      </dl>
      <p class="srcline">
        Sources: {p.sources.map((s: any, i: number) => (
          <>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.label}</a></>
        ))}
      </p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>What is not here</h2>
  <p class="meta">
    Absence is a claim too, so these are stated as specifically as the presences above.
  </p>
  <ul class="absent">
    {notPresent.map((n: any) => (
      <li><strong>{n.what}.</strong> {n.detail}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the documentation runs out</h2>
  <p>
    We file freedom-of-information requests because organisations do not always publish what
    they do. The same problem applies to the companies we depend on, and pretending otherwise
    would be the overclaim that gets a site like this caught. Three gaps, stated plainly:
  </p>
  <ul class="gaps">
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare does not publish a retention period.</strong> Its privacy policy gives
      criteria, not durations. The only concrete figure it has published is in a blog post from
      2013 that no current documentation restates, so we do not cite it as current practice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google does not document a default retention period</strong> for a newly created
      Analytics property. Two options exist, 2 months and 14 months. The claim that new
      properties default to the shorter one is repeated widely but we could not trace it to
      Google's own documentation, so it is not on this page as fact.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenFreeMap does not address tile-request logging.</strong> Its policy covers IP
      addresses clearly and commits to keeping anonymised logs indefinitely, but does not say
      whether the record of which map areas were viewed is among them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare does not document whether its email-obfuscation script collects
      anything.</strong> Its documentation describes what the feature does and never addresses
      data or cookies, so what we say about that script above comes from reading it ourselves
      rather than from a commitment anyone is bound to.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="meta">
    We found the fourth of these by comparing the files we published against what the live site
    actually served, and we would not have found it any other way. That is worth stating on a
    page like this: the gap between what an organisation believes it runs and what it actually
    runs is not usually malice, which is exactly why nobody should be asked to take it on trust.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Corrections wanted.</strong> If any operator above documents something that
    contradicts this page, send the link and we will correct it and say that we did.
    <a href="mailto:themiltonrecord@gmail.com">themiltonrecord@gmail.com</a>
    <span class="asof">Every claim on this page was checked against its source on {verified}.</span>
  </div>

  <style>
    .party {
      background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 1rem 1.15rem; margin: 0.9rem 0;
    }
    .party-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.6rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .party h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 0; }
    .scope {
      font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--muted);
      border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.05rem 0.5rem;
    }
    .need {
      font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.05rem 0.5rem;
    }
    .need.required { color: var(--toll); background: rgba(77,163,255,0.14); }
    .need.chosen { color: var(--fleet); background: rgba(255,181,77,0.14); }
    .need.default { color: var(--border-cat); background: rgba(176,122,255,0.14); }
    .tests { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); gap: 0.7rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
    .test { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 0.8rem 0.95rem; }
    .test h3 { font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0 0 0.3rem; color: var(--text); }
    .test p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
    .close {
      border-left: 3px solid var(--toll); padding-left: 0.9rem;
      color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 1rem 0 1.6rem;
    }
    .role { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0.4rem 0 0.7rem; }
    .party dl { margin: 0; }
    .party dt {
      font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.7rem;
    }
    .party dd { margin: 0.2rem 0 0; color: var(--text); font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .party blockquote {
      margin: 0.5rem 0 0; padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--toll);
      background: var(--panel-2); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
      color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.86rem;
    }
    .party blockquote cite { display: block; margin-top: 0.25rem; font-size: 0.78rem; font-style: normal; }
    .retention { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; }
    .retention.undocumented { color: var(--fleet); }
    .detail { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.86rem; }
    .control-links {
      display: block; margin-top: 0.4rem; font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .control-links a { color: var(--toll); text-decoration: none; }
    .control-links a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .control-note { border-left-color: var(--fleet); }
    .our-claim {
      margin-top: 0.55rem; padding: 0.55rem 0.8rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--fleet);
      background: var(--panel-2); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
      color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.86rem;
    }
    .our-claim strong { color: var(--fleet); }
    .srcline { margin-top: 0.8rem; font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--muted); }
    .srcline a { color: var(--toll); text-decoration: none; }
    .srcline a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; }
    .absent, .gaps { margin: 0.8rem 0 1.4rem; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
    .absent li, .gaps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .absent strong, .gaps strong { color: var(--text); }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.45rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.78rem; }
    tr.us td { background: rgba(255,181,77,0.07); }
    .asof { display: block; margin-top: 0.35rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); }
  </style>
</Base>
